-- `@inline(always)` - a body expanded where it is called, and never compiled into a function.
--
-- What the fixture is about is `main`: every one of these calls is gone from it, replaced by the
-- instructions of the body with the arguments substituted for the parameters. The file is compiled
-- with the optimizer off, so what is printed is what the *resolver* produced rather than what a
-- later pass recovered - which is the whole reason this is not left to the inliner.
--
-- The bodies are still printed here because this is the root module, whose functions are all
-- printed whether or not anything calls them. In a library they are not: `Function::used` stays
-- false for a callee every site expanded, which is what keeps `null` out of every program that
-- mentions it. `nowhere`'s specialization is not printed even here, since it belongs to the module
-- that declared the generic rather than to this one.

import Native

@inline(always)
fn add1(x: Int) -> Int = x + 1

-- Two calls, so the expansion is not a one-off: each gets its own copy of the body's one
-- instruction, and the constant is re-created per site rather than shared across functions.
@inline(always)
fn scaled(x: Int, by: Int) -> Int = x * by + 1

-- The argument arrives already converted to the declared type, exactly as it would have for a
-- call, so an expansion is never where a conversion is skipped.
@inline(always)
fn widened(x: U8) -> Int = x :: Int

-- A generic one. Its body is a `gencall` until the type arguments are known - `Bitcast` is a class -
-- so what the site grafts is the specialization, and the specialization is not emitted either. This
-- is `lib/Native`'s own `null`, written out here so the fixture does not depend on the library
-- keeping the attribute.
@inline(always)
fn nowhere() -> %a = bitcast(0 :: Size)

-- A body whose value is one of its own parameters, which is the case that hands back a value this
-- expansion did not create: nothing is copied and the caller's argument is the result.
@inline(always)
fn same(x: Int) -> Int = x

fn main() -> Int:
    let a = add1(1)
    let b = scaled(a, 3)
    let c = widened(4 :: U8)
    let p = nowhere() :: %U8
    let d = same(b + c)
    return if isNull(p) then a + d else 0
